  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nOur deep upper low responsible for this very active and rainy \npattern continues move east across the southern High Plains, \nsteadily weakening as it does. At the surface, low pressure is over \nsouthwest KS, beginning to drop southeast as a cold front pushes \ninto north-central KS. We continue to see heavy showers redevelop \nalong and behind this frontal boundary. Mentioned in yesterday's \ndiscussion that Tuesday evening's sounding at KTOP showed 2.05\" of \nPWAT, tied for second highest on record for any sounding after \nSeptember 25. Well Wednesday evening's sounding took that up a notch \nwith 2.22\" of PWAT, highest on record after 9/25. Very telling for \nhow efficient some of these storms have been at producing rain. Many \nplaces across northern KS from Sabetha to Concordia have already \nseen 2.5-5\" of rain over the past 36 hours.\n\nAs the front pushes farther southeast throughout the day, expecting \nadditional showers and weak thunderstorms to develop along and \nbehind the boundary. Similar to the past couple days, the high \nmoisture and repeated generation of convection may result in locally \nheavy rain, including areas of east-central KS that haven't seen \nquite as much yet. Expecting an additional 0.5 to 1.5\" for most \nplaces, with isolated totals of around 3\". The front looks to fully \nclear the area by 0z, allowing rain coverage and intensity to \nsteadily wane over the evening as brisk northeast winds advect drier \nair southwards.\n\nThe remainder of the forecast period looks a beautiful and quiet \nstretch of October weather as northwesterly flow persists across the \nCentral Plains. On the periphery of strong Canadian high pressure, \nstrong surface ridging moves across our area Friday into early \nSaturday and again Sunday into early Monday. This will keep \ntemperatures a bit cooler than average through Monday. Mostly clear \nskies will allow highs to climb into the upper 60s to mid 70s each \nday, with crisp overnight lows in the 40s to low 50s. Does look like \nwe'll see a bit of a warming trend for the middle part of next week \nas upper ridging strengthens over the central Rockies, but dry \nweather should persist.",
